1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
So how does its loneliness fit into my 20 s something canundrum.
那么，这种孤独又怎样融入她二十多岁的困惑呢？〔its、canundrum 原字幕识别待复核〕

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:06,600
Well being in your 20 s is already a very lonely time.
二十多岁本来就是一段很孤独的时期。

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,120
For some people it's the first time you move away from home.
对一些人来说，这是第一次搬离家。

4
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:11,439
For others you might still live in the city you grew up in.
另一些人也许仍住在自己长大的城市。

5
00:00:11,439 --> 00:00:13,630
But you're less reliant on your family now.
但你现在对家人的依赖更少了。

6
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,199
And while social media is a great way to stay connected.
虽然社交媒体是保持联系的好方法——

7
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:18,960
It doesn't replace the personal connections you have in your real life.
它却不能替代现实生活中的个人联结。

8
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:24,120
And I think sometimes we forget that in generations before while it was still difficult to make friends in a new city.
她觉得，有时我们忘了：在以前的世代，去新城市交朋友同样不容易——

9
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:26,640
It didn't feel as impossible as it does now.
但似乎不像现在这样，让人觉得几乎不可能。

10
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:32,640
And why do I make that claim because literally everyone I know has had a parent or just an older person in general.
她为什么这样说？因为她认识的每个人，都听父母或某个年长者说过——

11
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:38,280
Tell them how they just need to get out there just get out there you you'll meet a boyfriend you'll make some friends.
你只要走出去，走出去，就会遇到男朋友、交到朋友。

12
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:42,160
Which makes sense they would say that because when they were younger that's exactly how you did it.
他们这样说也合理，因为他们年轻时确实就是那样交朋友的。

13
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:44,240
But now that's not so common anymore.
但现在，这样的情况已不那么常见。

14
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,120
My friend recently told me after she took a flight that her mom told her before she went to the airport.
最近她朋友坐过一次飞机后告诉她，妈妈在她去机场前叮嘱——

15
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:53,640
That she should look nice in case she meets someone there romantically on the airplane.
她应该打扮好看一些，以防在飞机上遇到有浪漫可能的人。

16
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:57,600
Because her mom one of her boyfriends on an airplane.
因为她妈妈和某个前男友的相识与飞机有关。〔原字幕此处缺少动词，待原声复核，不补写英语〕

17
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:02,799
And maybe that can still happen but I don't think I've ever even spoken to someone on an airplane to be quite honest.
也许这种事仍会发生，但老实说，她甚至觉得自己从没在飞机上和人交谈过。

18
00:01:02,799 --> 00:01:08,559
Aside from like the flight attendant and maybe the person next to me for like 1 second when I had to go to the bathroom.
除了空乘，或者要去洗手间时，和旁边的人说上一秒钟的话。

19
00:01:08,559 --> 00:01:10,760
Or something it's just not that easy.
之类的。事情就是没有那么容易。

20
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:15,640
There's a common cons sentence I've been seeing online right now that we just need more third spaces.
她最近常在网上看到一种说法：我们只是需要更多“第三空间”。〔cons sentence 识别疑点，待复核〕

21
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:22,240
But the thing is you actually need to go to the third space to you know keep the third space alive.
但问题是，你必须真的去那个空间，才能让它持续存在。

22
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:27,480
And also to contribute to the third space community which people don't do because they feel too awkward too.
也要为那个空间的社群作贡献；而人们因为觉得太尴尬，反而不去做这些。

23
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:33,040
A few months ago I was at work and this girl came in and she was looking to see if we had community groups.
几个月前她上班时，一个女孩进来，想看看这里是否有社群活动小组。

24
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:38,679
I'm trying not to give away where I work um but community groups like some of the other businesses in our area had.
她不想暴露自己的工作地点；总之，女孩问的是附近其他商家也会有的那种社群小组。

25
00:01:38,679 --> 00:01:44,640
I told her no but then we got to talking and she explained that she was new to the area and that she wanted to make some friends.
她回答没有，但后来两人聊起来，女孩解释说自己刚来这里，想交些朋友。

26
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:48,920
So even though I felt kind of weird about it I gave this random girl my instagram.
所以，尽管她觉得有点奇怪，还是把自己的 Instagram 给了这个素不相识的女孩。

27
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:51,640
And then we ended up hanging out and having a really good time.
后来她们约着出去玩，度过了很愉快的时光。

28
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:54,640
Yeah it was kind of awkward but also it was pretty fun.
是有一点尴尬，但也挺开心。

29
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:58,880
Eventually she ended up moving away and we haven't really kept in contact you could say.
后来女孩搬走了，也可以说，她们没有怎么继续保持联系。

30
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:02,120
But it was still fun to meet someone in the wild and hang out with them.
但在现实中遇到一个人，再一起玩，仍然很有趣。

31
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:07,760
My point of that story isn't to flex like oh you're all losers I can make friends because no I think that was like.
她讲这件事并不是炫耀，说“你们都不行，只有我会交朋友”，因为并不是这样，她觉得那算是——

32
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:11,480
One of the only people in the last 5 years that I've met like that.
过去五年中少数几个通过这种方式认识的人之一。

33
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:15,110
Like that I didn't have any prior connection to and then we hung out.
也就是事先没有任何联系，后来却一起出去玩的人。

34
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:21,000
But instead I tell it as a word of encouragement that it is possible to meet someone and become friends with them in the wild.
她讲这件事，是想给人一点鼓励：在现实中遇见一个人，并成为朋友，是可能的。

35
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,640
It's a two way street though you have to be willing to put yourself out there.
不过这是双向的，你必须愿意主动迈出去。

36
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:28,880
As much as you have to be willing to accept someone who is putting themselves out there.
同时也要愿意接纳那个正在主动迈向你的人。

37
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:33,160
It's just frustrating to see when people do go out in public with the intention of making friends.
让人沮丧的是，有人带着交朋友的意图出门，来到公共场所——

38
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:35,720
And then literally no one gives them the time of day.
可根本没人愿意搭理他们。

39
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:40,080
So then they end up just going home and going back to their phone and their comfort.
于是他们又回家，回到手机和舒适区。

40
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:43,080
Because clearly no one wants to be friends in the real world anymore.
因为看起来，现实世界里已经没人想交朋友了。

41
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:46,520
I also think sometimes people don't realize how much social interaction they're lacking.
她还觉得，有时人们意识不到自己缺少了多少社交互动。

42
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:51,880
Because they spend so much time on their phone and they feel like they're getting that social interaction even though they're not.
因为他们花太多时间在手机上，觉得自己获得了社交互动，实际上却未必如此。

43
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:55,240
I'm not talking about like online friends there because that's obvious social interaction.
她在这里说的不是网上的朋友，因为那显然属于社交互动。

44
00:02:55,240 --> 00:03:00,960
But I mean like just scrolling and watching videos of people and feeling like that parasocial relationship with them.
她指的是刷着别人发的视频，感觉自己和他们形成了某种单向的拟社会关系。

45
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:06,080
Growing up in an internet generation I sometimes worry what would happen if I did cut social media out of my life.
作为在互联网时代长大的人，她有时担心：如果把社交媒体从生活里去掉，会怎样？

46
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:11,030
Because I think it would feel like I was cutting out one of my main sources of social interaction.
因为那会像是切断了自己一个主要的社交来源。

47
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:15,669
But being young and alive I think I need to let that idea go even if it means.
但既然年轻、活着，她觉得自己需要放下这种想法，即使意味着——

48
00:03:15,679 --> 00:03:18,640
Having to talk to my friends less on a daily basis.
每天与朋友说话的次数变少。

49
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:22,799
I think that would be better for our relationship in general cuz we would have to call each other.
她觉得那总体上可能对彼此的关系更好，因为她们得真正打电话联系。

50
00:03:22,799 --> 00:03:25,640
And being more intentional with the time time we spend with each other.
也会更有意识地对待彼此相处的时间。〔time time 原字幕重复，待复核〕

51
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:29,640
Rather than taking it for granted because oh I can just send them a text about this later.
而不是想当然地觉得：哦，反正之后给她们发条消息说这事就行了。
